\DOC LOCAL_MONTMUL_P256_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_MONTMUL_P256_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually}-style machine-execution goal over one inlined
Montgomery-multiplication subroutine call, replacing the current symbolic state
by a successor state and adding the arithmetic specification of the computed
result.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} offset/index used to locate the call site within
the program; the second is a {string list} naming, in order, the output buffer
and the two input buffers of the multiplication, which identifies the
stack/argument slots the step reads and writes. The assumptions {A} of the goal
describe a symbolic state: a program-counter value, a {bytes_loaded} fact,
register contents, memory contents of the relevant buffers, and a cumulative
{MAYCHANGE} frame relating the initial state to {s}.
{
   A ?- eventually ... s
   ================================================  LOCAL_MONTMUL_P256_TAC n ["out"; "in1"; "in2"]
   A' ?- eventually ... s'
}
\noindent Here {A'} is {A} with all state-indexed facts about {s} discarded and
the corresponding facts about {s'} added: a new program-counter equation, the
{bytes_loaded} fact, the register and memory-content equations re-expressed at
{s'} (with the designated output buffer now holding a new variable {z1}), a
{MAYCHANGE} fact for {s'} extended by the written memory region, and one new
arithmetic assumption of implicational form {t ==> u} stating that, under a
bound on the product of the two inputs, {z1} equals the Montgomery product of
the inputs modulo the field prime. Purely mathematical assumptions not
mentioning a state are retained unchanged, though they may be reordered.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces, the tactic performs this single-successor-step
behaviour on goals whose assumptions already carry such a symbolic state
description.

\ENDDOC
